{"_id":"vuQKJsnq1WxPiDLU","name":"King Maalva","type":"npc","img":"icons/svg/mystery-man.svg","system":{"characteristics":{"brawn":{"value":2},"agility":{"value":2},"intellect":{"value":2},"cunning":{"value":2},"willpower":{"value":2},"presence":{"value":2}},"stats":{"wounds":{"value":0,"max":10},"strain":{"value":0,"max":10},"soak":{"value":0},"defence":{"melee":0,"ranged":0}},"biography":{"description":"A nephew of the deceased Katuunko, Maalva succeeded Katuunko after his assassination during the Clone Wars. Maalva does not share his uncle’s mild temperament—he is a cantankerous, conniving, petty braggart. Nevertheless, he manages to retain his throne through political savvy and by recruiting ambitious vassals who pledge their loyalty to him. Rather than use his power to better his kingdom, Maalva lines his own pockets and blames the lack of prosperity on the Empire, the Hutts, and a bad roll of the chance cube.\n\nKing Maalva tolerates the Imperials by order of his Hutt overlords, but he also has gained personal benefit from their presence. Unbeknownst to his subjects and even his councilmen, the king orders the abduction of Toydarian villagers in far-flung settlements and sells them into Imperial slavery. In return, Maalva is paid handsomely, and starships bearing the royal seal are exempt from all customs inspections."}},"items":[{"_id":"VU72kTpYDNhTe3SN","name":"Ceremonial sword","type":"weapon","system":{"damage":0,"critical":3,"range":"Engaged","skill":"Melee","qualities":""}},{"_id":"CY3M7MeVp9QDEHxK","name":"Talent","type":"talent","system":{"description":"","activation":"Passive","tier":1,"ranked":false}},{"_id":"nL5MMYzpKG5RZs6G","name":"Talent","type":"talent","system":{"description":"","activation":"Passive","tier":1,"ranked":false}},{"_id":"ar40jwGDDFD0PxLJ","name":"Talent","type":"talent","system":{"description":"","activation":"Passive","tier":1,"ranked":false}}],"effects":[],"flags":{},"ownership":{"default":0}}
